@*
    The run's per-config breakdown, so an escalation from a 4b to a 26b is visible instead of
    averaged away.

    Revealed at run end, and only there (decision 04): mid-run the breakdown is incomplete and
    moving, so it is noise; at run end it is the actual comparison the per-config decision exists to
    serve. A run that never escalated renders a one-row table — honest and cheap. Deliberately not
    always-visible chips and not a <details> expander, both of which were prototyped and rejected.

    Pull, not push: it reads a snapshot handed to it by whoever is already painting, subscribes to
    nothing and starts no timer (decision 06).
*@
@if (Snapshot is { HasRun: true, IsRunActive: false, PerConfig.Count: > 0 })
{
    <MudSimpleTable Dense="true" Hover="false" Elevation="0" Class="mt-2">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Config</th>
                <th>tok/s</th>
                <th>req</th>
                <th>tokens</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var row in Snapshot.PerConfig)
            {
                @* Keyed by config id, never by name: a config renamed mid-run stays one row, and
                   the name is only ever a label resolved at render. *@
                <tr @key="row.ConfigId">
                    <td>@row.ConfigName</td>
                    <td style="@ThroughputText.StyleFor(row.TokensPerSecond)">@ThroughputText.BareRate(row.TokensPerSecond)</td>
                    <td>@row.Requests</td>
                    <td>@ThroughputText.Count(row.TokensOut)</td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </MudSimpleTable>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The run's figures, pulled by the surface that is painting.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public ThroughputSnapshot Snapshot { get; set; } = ThroughputSnapshot.Empty;
}
